# Video scripts for Module 3 (Intensive Newborn Care). Narration from the manual; (FG) = facilitator guide.
S = {}
S['SN3.1.1'] = dict(
    title='Pulse oximetry: detecting hypoxaemia in a newborn', length='2 minutes',
    film=['A newborn with fast breathing and nasal flaring. Close-ups of the clinical signs: central cyanosis, nasal flaring, grunting, indrawing, head nodding.',
          'Placing a neonatal pulse oximeter probe on the right hand or foot; waiting for a steady trace; reading SpO₂ and heart rate.'],
    narration=[
        'Hypoxaemia means low levels of oxygen in the blood. It is a life-threatening condition that requires early detection and treatment.',
        'Clinical signs: central cyanosis, nasal flaring, grunting with every breath, and a depressed mental state. Less specific signs: fast breathing of 70 a minute or more, severe lower chest wall indrawing, and head nodding.',
        'Pulse oximetry is the most accurate non-invasive method for detecting hypoxaemia. It measures the percentage of oxygenated haemoglobin in arterial blood, the SpO₂.',
        'The normal SpO₂ at sea level is 97 to 99%. In practice, oxygen is given when the SpO₂ is below 90%: small falls below 90% may be a dangerous fall in arterial oxygen.'],
    note='The probe-placement steps are in the Module 2 lesson "Pulse oximeter" (standard operating procedure); narrate them from there when filming.')
S['SN3.1.2'] = dict(
    title='Giving oxygen by nasal prongs and nasal catheter', length='3 minutes',
    film=['Nasal prongs: choosing the size, placing the prongs into the nostrils, securing with tape on the cheeks near the nose; setting the flowmeter to 0.5–1 L/min.',
          'Nasal catheter: measuring from the side of the nostril to the inner margin of the eyebrow, inserting an 8F catheter, checking that the tip is not visible below the uvula.'],
    narration=[
        'Nasal prongs: standard flow rates are 0.5 to 1 litre per minute for neonates. Humidification is not required at these flows; the nose heats and humidifies the oxygen.',
        'The distal prong should fit well into the nostril: 1 mm in premature infants. Secure the prongs with a piece of tape on the cheeks near the nose.',
        'In infants up to 10 kg, flows of 0.5, 1 and 2 litres per minute give an FiO₂ of about 35, 45 and 55%.',
        'A nasal catheter is a thin, flexible tube passed into the nose, ending in the nasal cavity. Use an 8-French catheter in neonates and infants.',
        'Passed for a distance equal to the distance from the side of the nostril to the inner margin of the eyebrow, it usually reaches the back of the nasal cavity. The tip should not be visible below the uvula.',
        'Nasopharyngeal catheters should only be used when nasal prongs are unavailable: they can be displaced into the oesophagus and need effective humidification.'])
S['SN3.2.1'] = dict(
    title='Setting up bubble CPAP step by step', length='4 minutes',
    film=['The CPAP circuit: air and oxygen connected, blender, humidifier filled with distilled water, inspiratory and expiratory tubing, expiratory limb in the water bottle.',
          'The baby: prong size chosen (the biggest that fits without distending), cap fitted with ears flat, barrier dressing, prongs moistened with saline, secured without touching the columella; orogastric tube passed.',
          'Settings: pressure 5 cm H₂O, flow 5 L/min, FiO₂ 30%; continuous bubbling in both phases of breathing; SpO₂ on the monitor.'],
    narration=[
        'CPAP applies a constant distending pressure during inhalation and exhalation to support a spontaneously breathing newborn.',
        'It improves the functional residual capacity and so decreases the work of breathing; it splints the airway, conserves surfactant and decreases apnoea.',
        'The parts: a flow generator of warm, humidified, blended air and oxygen; a positive pressure generator; a humidifier; and the nasal interface.',
        'In bubble CPAP, the pressure comes from placing the end of the expiratory tubing under water. The pressure is set by the depth of the tube under the surface.',
        'Nasal CPAP is usually started at a pressure of 5 cm H₂O, a flow of 5 litres per minute and an FiO₂ of 30%.',
        'Then titrate: the pressure to reduce the work of breathing, the flow to obtain continuous bubbling in both phases of breathing, and the FiO₂ to reach the saturation target, 90 to 95% for preterm neonates.',
        'Use an orogastric rather than a nasogastric tube, so the nostrils are not distorted and the prongs seal well.',
        '(FG) A poorly fitted CPAP causes nasal injury, air leaks and treatment failure. The fit is the therapy.'])
S['SN3.2.2'] = dict(
    title='Caring for a baby on CPAP: the hourly and four-hourly checks', length='3 minutes',
    film=['A nurse at the bedside of a baby on CPAP; clock on screen.',
          'Hourly visual check: nose not squashed or pushed up, eyes visible, tapes not too tight.',
          'Four-hourly hands-on check: hat fit, prongs removed by loosening the tapes, nose inspected and scored, prongs cleaned, ears checked; mouth care; documentation.'],
    narration=[
        'Every hour, check visually: the nose should not be squashed or pushed upwards, the eyes should be clearly visible, and the tapes should not be too tight.',
        'At least every four hours, check physically: the hat should be the right fit; the prongs or mask are removed to rest the nose; the nose is inspected for redness, skin breakdown, bruising, indentation, altered shape and bleeding.',
        'Check that the prongs are clean and patent before replacing them. Inspect the ears: not creased or folded, no skin breakdown.',
        'Remove the prongs or mask by loosening the tapes, never by pulling them straight off the face. Give regular mouth care. Suction is not routine, only as the baby needs.',
        'Score the nose and document it. If the nose has deteriorated, inform the nurse in charge and the medical staff.',
        'Every baby on CPAP should have their blood pressure checked at least six-hourly.'])
S['SN3.3.1'] = dict(
    title='Reading ventilator waveforms', length='3 minutes', kind='animation',
    film=['Animated pressure, flow and volume curves, with the manual\'s figures as the guide.',
          'Mark the phases: trigger, peak inspiratory flow, cycling, peak expiratory flow, end of deflation.',
          'Show a spontaneous breath (negative pressure wave) and a mandatory breath (positive, square wave); then CMV (no negative waves) and IMV (negative waves between mandatory breaths), with the synchronisation window.'],
    narration=[
        'All ventilators provide four functions: they start lung inflation (triggering), inflate the lungs with a tidal volume (inspiration), end the inflation (cycling), and allow the lungs to empty (exhalation).',
        'Inspiratory time is the period from the trigger to the end of inspiration. Expiratory time runs from there to the next breath.',
        'A breath can be triggered by the machine or by the baby. The trigger may be pressure, volume, flow or time.',
        'A spontaneous breath is triggered and cycled by the patient. Its pressure wave is negative.',
        'A mandatory breath is one whose end of inspiration is determined by the ventilator. It may be patient-triggered or machine-triggered. Its pressure wave is positive.',
        'In continuous mandatory ventilation, all breaths are mandatory and no spontaneous breaths occur between them. In intermittent mandatory ventilation, spontaneous breaths occur between mandatory breaths; when these are synchronised to the patient\'s effort, it is SIMV.'])
S['SN3.3.2'] = dict(
    title='Extubating a preterm baby step by step', length='4 minutes',
    film=['A ventilated preterm on low settings. On screen: MAP 8 or less, FiO₂ below 30–40%, rate below 30 with an acceptable gas.',
          'Caffeine loading dose recorded; feeds stopped 4 hours before; CPAP circuit set up and bubbling at the bedside.',
          'Extubation by a skilled team; CPAP prongs placed immediately; monitoring after.'],
    narration=[
        'Have a written mechanical ventilation protocol, including weaning, extubation and reintubation criteria, agreed by neonatologists, nurses and respiratory therapists.',
        'Allow permissive hypercapnia, PaCO₂ 50 to 60 mmHg, and set oxygen saturation targets of 91 to 95%.',
        'Give a loading dose of caffeine before extubation, ideally within 24 hours before.',
        'Stop enteral feeds for 4 hours before extubation.',
        'Acceptable weaning parameters may include a mean airway pressure of 8 cm H₂O or less, FiO₂ less than 30 to 40%, and a rate less than 30 with an acceptable blood gas.',
        'Place the infant on CPAP or NIPPV immediately after extubation, PEEP 5 cm H₂O or more, using bi-nasal prongs or a nasal mask.',
        '(FG) Have the CPAP set up and running before you pull the tube.'])
S['SN3.3.3'] = dict(
    title='Sudden desaturation on the ventilator: disconnect and bag', length='2 minutes',
    film=['Simulation: a ventilated 27-week baby (manikin); the monitor shows SpO₂ falling to 78% and CO₂ rising.',
          'The nurse disconnects the ventilator and bags by hand.',
          'Ending A: bagging is easy, the baby pinks up; the team checks the oxygen source, FiO₂, circuit and the tube.',
          'Ending B: bagging is hard, the baby stays blue; the team transilluminates the chest and prepares to decompress.'],
    narration=[
        'Sudden desaturation with a high CO₂: disconnect the patient and bag.',
        'If he improves, check the ventilator: the oxygen source, increase the FiO₂, consider mechanical or electrical failure. Also check the tube for displacement, obstruction or leak.',
        'If he does not improve, assess the baby for pneumothorax, hypoglycaemia, hypotension, intraventricular haemorrhage and sepsis.',
        'Gradual desaturation with a high CO₂ suggests inappropriate ventilator settings: increase PIP, PEEP or the I:E ratio, and repeat the blood gas.',
        '(FG) Sudden means something broke: disconnect and bag. Gradual means the settings drifted from the lung: adjust and re-gas.'])
